---\par
The mission objective is, simply enough, to deal with every troll camp, or their leaders at the very least, on the area, one way or another, and then move through the northwest tunnel to the next mission. The first two camps recruit only troll whelps, and they don't have much money anyway, so they should be easy enough to deal with. The second two have trolls and troll rocklobbers, and the final one, which starts out as neutral, recruits troll heroes and shamans. The three villages in the immediate vicinity belong to the player by default.\par
\par
Once you encounter the first troll, of any type or rank, on the area:\par
Troll: "Wow, dere is whole army coming up. No good trying to get money from them, methinks."\par
\par
When a saurian engages in combat with an enemy:\par
Saurian: "Ha! No more snow, or cold, or wet! Just the firm rock ground under my toes and deep darkness around me!"\par
Saurian: "This was totally worth waiting for!"\par
\par
There's some hidden gold under every troll keep. The first two give twenty pieces each, the the second two fifty. The final castle has no money, though.\par
Unit stepping on the keep: "Hey, I found the troll's gold cache! I can count [amount] pieces of gold!\par
(If the unit picking them up is a saurian, then there's three gold pieces less. Thieving buggers.)\par
\par
It's also possible for Cheska to enter the exit before all trolls on the area have been dealt with. If the player tries this, dialogue happens:\par
Cheska: "We cannot leave yet. We have a pact with dwarves, and breaking it would be extremely poor form."\par
\par
Aside from that, there isn't much dialogue for most of the mission, until you reach the final troll camp:\par
Great troll: "Who goes dere?"\par
Cheska: "I am Cheska, and I'm coming through!"\par
Troll: "Not without paying a toll you won't! I take five gold from each of you!"\par
Cheska: "Oh, I'm sorry, but I don't think this will work that way. You see, we are under contract by dwarves."\par
Troll: "Dorfs? Did dorfs send you?"\par
Cheska: "We could pay you and be on our way, but then you would just keep charging from dwarves to use their own tunnels."\par
Cheska: "That won't do. We will have to remove you from here."\par
Troll: "You gonna remove us?"\par
Cheska: "Yes."\par
(Beat.)\par
Troll: "You got an army. This gonna get ugly. For both of us."\par
Cheska: "Possibly. Do you have something in mind?"\par
Troll: "Pay two hundred gold. We leave. Go bully orcs, maybe."\par
Cheska: "Two hundred?"\par
\par
That's the price for normal difficulty. On easy, it's just one hundred; on hard, three hundred. The player has two options, each of which branch on two additional outcomes depending on whether Lukas is still alive or not.\par
\par
If you have enough money and decide to pay them:\par
Cheska: "Your price is sufficient. I will pay, and then you will leave."\par
Troll: "Great! Pleasure doing business with you!"\par
And after a short fade-to-black, all trolls are gone: not only this particular, but also any other troll you may have left alive before this.\par
\par
If you refuse to pay, or don't have the money:\par
Cheska: "I am sorry, but your price is too high for me."\par
Troll: "Den we have to deal with this old-fashioned way! Come get me!"\par
And of course, if there are still any other trolls left on the area, you will have to finish them off too after this battle.\par
\par
When the great troll engages in combat, he says one more thing:\par
Troll: "You better surrender! My magic amulet protects me: you can't win!"\par
Cheska: "A magic amulet, you say?"\par
\par
But actually, the troll falls quite a bit easier than he normally should have:\par
Elric: "He seemed rather weak to me, really."\par
Elric: "And he dropped something."\par
There is now an amulet on the place of the great troll, which you can pick up. More on it later.\par
\par
If you refuse to pay, even though you have enough money, and Lukas is in your party:\par
Cheska: "I am sorry, but your price is too-"\par
