Cheska: "Um. What's with that amulet, exactly?"\par
Lukas: "It has an unknown power in it. I cannot exactly say what it does, but it seems I was correct in my assumption that a troll is a wrong creature to wear it."\par
Cheska: "Do you... do you think I should try?"\par
Lukas: "Perhaps. I do not know what happens, though: try at your own risk."\par
\par
Regardless of what happens, there should now be a magic amulet lying nearby - unless you paid for the trolls and didn't have Lukas with you. When an unit tries to pick it up:\par
Unit: "This is the amulet that made the troll leader weaker. I wonder what effect it would have over me...?"\par
If the unit is lawful: "It feels... I feel weird. But stronger, somehow! Fearless!"\par
If the unit is neutral (I don't think you have any): "Hmm. There is no effect. I think I will keep this with me, to see whether it will affect in time."\par
If the unit is chaotic (likely a saurian): "Ohh... I feel rather dizzy. It's really strange."\par
\par
The amulet is, in fact, called Pendant of Radiance, and it grants both its wearer and the opponent the permanent ability to fight as if it was always day. So it's obviously the most effective when it's used by a lawful unit who fights a chaotic, while being worn by a chaotic creature is actually detrimental. The game doesn't tell any of this: you have to figure it out yourself.\par
\par
